====Usage notes====
* Unlike {{m|en|etc.}}, ''e.g.'' is very seldom read as a full Latin phrase. Like {{m|en|i.e.}}, it is typically read out as its English {{glossary|calque}} (“[[for example]]”) or as its letters (“E-G”). It is also sometimes taught or glossed as “example given” for the benefit of English speakers.
* ''E.g.'' and its examples are typically set off from the rest of the sentence by punctuation. In US English, ''e.g.'' is usually followed by a comma.<ref>{{cite-web|author={{w|Mignon Fogarty}}|title=Grammar Girl: I.e. Versus E.g.|work=Quick and Dirty Tips|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230525210405/https://www.quickanddirtytips.com/articles/i-e-versus-e-g/|date=20 October 2016}}.</ref> It is not followed by a comma in other English-speaking countries.
:: {{q|UK}} ''I like sweet foods, e.g. [[danish]]es.''
:: {{q|US}} ''I like sweet foods (e.g., [[marzipan]]) but brush regularly.''
:: {{q|US}} ''I like sweet foods (e.g. [[marzipan]]) but brush regularly.''
:: {{q|rare|US}} ''I like sweet foods — e.g., [[red bean|red-bean]] [[zongzi]] — and so prefer [[Shanghainese]] cuisine to, e.g., [[Cantonese]].''
* The example(s) following ''e.g.'' should be illustrative, ''not'' exhaustive.<ref>{{cite-book|author=Ernest Gowers|authorlink=Ernest Gowers|author2=Sidney Greenbaum|authorlink2=Sidney Greenbaum|author3=Janet Whitcut|title=The Complete Plain Words|edition=2nd U.S.|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher={{w|Godine}}|year=2002|isbn=978-1-56792-203-5}}.</ref> An exhaustive list or rephrasing uses ''i.e.'' instead. The use of {{m|en|etc.}} after ''e.g.'' is typically redundant.
* Rarely, {{m|en|exempli gratia}} is spelled in full.

